ic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What investigations would you do"},{"label":"B","text":"What will be your fluid management be if his pulse 120/minute, BP 80/50, and you assess him as being severely dehydrated. He weighs 60kg."},{"label":"C","text":"How will you treat the patient if he is found to have free fluid in the abdomen and air under the diaphragm? Two sentences are enough to describe the treatment"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-945570b7a819","stem":"List the causes of peripheral limb gangrenes","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c355b5eb1e59","stem":"List the cardinal symptom and signs and simple investigation which you use to diagnose simple in complicated appendicitis. How do you administer antibiotics after simple uncomplicated appendicectomy.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-61e232b66ac8","stem":"List down four signs of healed fracture in a long bone (e.g. Femur)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b3fc44d5d40c","stem":"A child comes to your clinic with a fresh displaced supracondylar fracture humerus. What are the signs you will look for to rule out severed/compressed neurovascular bundle? (five signs). SECTION III - LONG ESSAY QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0908ac2ecdb3","stem":"The following is one of the early complications of burn: Cushing ulcer Marjolin ulcer Curling’s ulcer Tropical ulcer None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a76097729ed3","stem":"The following are correct about burn wound except: Partial thickness burns heals by reepithilialization Second degree burns heals with minimal scarring and are painless First degree burn involve only the epidermis and is characterized by erytheme Superficial burns are often characterized by blister formation None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f0f95e10240","stem":"The following are major burn injuries except: Second degree burn of > 25% BSA in adults Second degree burn of > 25% BSA in children Second degree burn of < 15% BSA in adult Third degree burn of > 10% BSA Burn injury complicated by other major trauma","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0568a1142b7b","stem":"Considering the Closed Method of burn wound management the following statements are true except: An occlusive dressing is applied over the burn area Dressing is usually changed twice daily There is less pain and less heat loss The bacterial growth is not enhanced None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-64bc8ba74e0b","stem":"The following are true above chest tube insertion: Is done just below the rib above in the intercostals space 5% lignocaine is used as local anaesthetic For haemothorax is inserted in 7th intercostals space in the midaxillary line For Empyema thracis is inserted in the 2nd intercostals space midaxillary line 1% xylocane may be used","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6cfacacb52e6","stem":"The following are true about pneumothorax except: May be spontaneous or following trauma Is associated with sudden chest pain Is characterized by hyperresonance May be associated with ventilatory and circulatory compromise If it is less than 25% will need tube thoracostomy","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0e2c05686779","stem":"The following items can be sterilized using chemical method: Surgical linen Esophagascope Dressing gauze Laryngascope ‘b’ and ‘d’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f912896024bc","stem":"The following method of sterilization is used in industries for Mass production of sterile medical products: Chemical Autoclaving Gamma radiation Hot air oven None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30c36c7f0140","stem":"Shock is defined as the circumstance of insufficient oxygen delivery to the tissues. Etiology of shock in surgical patients is: Vomiting Dirrhoea Burn injury All of the above All of the above except (b)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2a045dea1331","stem":"Hypovolemic shock can be seen in patients with the following conditions except: Intestinal obstruction low and high Burn injury > 30% Excessive sweating Ruptured visceral Moderate Head injury","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c79776d64041","stem":"One of the following sentences is not correct: Sterilization is the destruction all living microbes Disinfection is the removal of harmful microbes not usually including bacterial spores Sterilized equipment is essential for all procedures involving a break in atmosphere, skin and mucous membrane Surgical asepsis refers to practices that render and keep objects and areas free from micro-organisms Contamination is the process by which an instrument is soiled with micro organisms","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2deb54e427b","stem":"A 15 years old girl noticed a painless little puffiness around the left ankle which she says disappears after a night’s rest, only to return at a late stage of the next day. She reports that it has gradually progressed slowly. Denies any skin changes, pigmentation or ulceration. What is the most likely clinical diagnosis? Recurrent cellulites Lymphoedema praecox Filariasis of the Left leg Secondary lymphoedema None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-55c9ded136b5","stem":"Which of the following is true about secondary lymphoedema? May follow involvement of lymph channels or lymph nodes by neoplastic, inflammatory process or surgical procedure Tends to be localized and develops much more rapidly Recurrent cellulites is not uncommon All of the above Only ‘a’ and ‘b’ is true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0f74f6d28126","stem":"Which of the following statements regarding abscess is true? Pyaemic abscess is the commonest variety Cardinal features of acute inflammation are usually present Fluctuation test becomes negative when the pus becomes superficial Usually treatment with parenteral antibiotics is enough None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb09f75058ac","stem":"The following are examples of an acquired fistula except: Fistula-in-ano Thyroglossal fistula Arteriovenous fistula Branchial fistula All of the above are acquired fistulae","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c345a956bf29","stem":"The following is true about incarcerated hernia: Has compromised blood supply Is not reducible Is reducible There is intestinal obstruction None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-348657ffc5cb","stem":"The following type of hernia appears in the Hesselbach’s triangle: Richter hernia Fermoral hernia Indirect hernia Direct hernia Umblical hernia","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1c5ecad0019c","stem":"The following is true about groin hernias in females: Inguinal hernia is the commonest Fermoral hernia is the commonest Direct hernia is the most common Males are prone to develop fermoral hernia compared to females None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-768c7e9dde2d","stem":"The following is true about groin hernia: Direct inguinal hernia produce fewer symptoms than indirect hernia Indirect hernia is less likely to strangulate than the direct hernia There is no weakness of the transversalis fascia in direct inguinal hernia The direct hernia exits through the processus vaginalis All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce6eff73d0e7","stem":"A hernia that descends into the scrotum is most likely to be: Direct inguinal hernia Fermoral hernia Richters hernia Indirect inguinal hernia All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2ef705e0375f","stem":"The following factors may cause hernia reccurance after repair: Poor surgical technique Overlooking an indirect sac Failure to repair the fascial defect securely Defect in Collagen Synthesis All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-70f41900f2fd","stem":"The following are complications of acute appendicitis except: Perforation Peritonitis Abscess formation Pylephlebitis None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-274750a9b8d3","stem":"The following are present in acute appendicitis except: Abdominal pain in left iliac fossa Anorexia Nausea Vomiting All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ac6f024bc0d","stem":"The following complication of acute appendicitis is managed expectantly: Peritonitis Appendicular abscess Appendicular mass Perforation None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ebb18c1e9aad","stem":"The following aerobic bacteria is frequently isolated from perforated appendicitis: Bacteroides fragilis Other bacteroides species Lactobacilus Fusobactoerium species Escherichia coli","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-15e5594a5ba8","stem":"One of the following skin conditions is a pre-malignant condition: Naevus flammeus (port-wine stain) Capillary haemangioma Naevi Squamous cell papilloma All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b55923316d04","stem":"Papillomas are: Are simple overgrowths of all layers of the skin May arise from epithelial surfaces either the epidermis or the mucosa May be treated by excision or application of formaldehyde Are benign tumours of glandular tissues ‘a’ ‘b’ and ‘c’ are the correct answers","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5692482221c","stem":"The following is a vascular tumour: Haemangioma Dermoid cyst Trichilemmal (pillar) cyst Epidermal cyst All of the above are correct answers","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-831fdce9d878","stem":"These are cutaneous neural (neuromas) tumours except: Acrochordons (skin tags) Neurofibromas Neurilemmomas (Schwannomas) Granular cell tumours ‘a’ and ‘b’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-02b3e0548899","stem":"Aneurysm: Is defined as a degree of arterial narrowing sufficient to produce a pressure drop. Is an irreversible dilatation of an artery to at least one and one half times its diameter True aneurysm involves all layers of the arterial wall False aneurysm involves only a portion of the vessel wall or surrounding tissues All are correct except ‘a’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-31f30b514363","stem":"Arterial vasospasm occurs in all of the following clinical conditions except: Arteriosclerosis Raynaud’s syndrome Acrocyanosis Drug induced e.g. ergotamine, oral contraceptives Scleroderma","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-347e196c5f52","stem":"These are characteristic features of dry gangrene except: Deprivation of arterial blood supply Deprivation of venous drainage Venous outflow remains unimpeded Occlusion of a vessel is by a chronic process or an acute process Colour changes from greenish to finally black","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7857e18b7db","stem":"The following are the indications for amputation except: Loss of viability due to non-reconstructible ischaemia Trauma that is sufficient extensive to preclude repair Tumours Extensive infection that does not respond to conservative measures or cause septicaemia Established contracture of the limb","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ea6d145ec363","stem":"The following are present in patient with thyrotoxicosis except: Nervousness Heat intolerance Muscular weakness Polyuria None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50cce47570bf","stem":"In patients with hyperthyrodism the following may be present except: Goitre Warm moist skin Thyroid thrill and bruit Gynaecomastia None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cbeb016c22b1","stem":"With regard to the treatment of thyrotoxicosis, the following are effective options: Anthithyroid drugs Radioactive iodine Thyroidectomy All of the above None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-159911dcf1f0","stem":"An ulcer with Rolled edge is most likely to be: Epithelioma Rodent ulcer Tuberculous ulcer Healing ulcer All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-879a950ff14b","stem":"An abscess is a collection of pus. Bacteria which cause pus (Pyogenic Bacteria) reach the infected area by: Direct infection from outside Local extension from some adjacent focus of infection Lymphatics Blood stream All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-026bc9bd44a2","stem":"The following techniques may allow surgical infections and collection of pus to be accurately diagnosed and clearly defined: Conventional radiology Isotope scanning Ultrasound Computed Axial Tomography All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-53d7a7703060","stem":"The principles of antibiotic prophylaxis: Choose antibiotics for the expected type of contamination Use antibiotics only if the risk of infection justifies their use Give antibiotics in appropriate doses and at appropriate times Stop dosing before the risk of side effects outweighs benefits All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-19ed5f058bdf","stem":"Thrombosis of veins is predisposed to by Virchow’s triad. The following are the factors in the Virchow’s triad: Change in the vessel wall with damage to endothelium Increased coagulability of the blood Diminished rate of blood flow Anaemia ‘a’, ‘b’ and ‘c’ are correct","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a899057ad3b","stem":"The following are the complications of varicose vein except: Thrombophlebitis Chronic dermatitis (eczema) Venous ulcer All of the above None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-73ae7e42e75c","stem":"The best diagnostic procedure to differentiate mechanical small bowel obstruction from peritonitis: Abdominal distension Peristalsis reduction on auscultation A palpable left lower quadrant abdominal mass Dehydration Abdominal tympany SECTION II - SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9769666775ab","stem":"Total body water constitutes _______60_____% of the body weight. Where by the ___60________% times kg/body weight (70kg) is equivalent to ______420_ kgs = _______42_____ litres.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-04f699538326","stem":"List 5 classical local signs of inflammation: __________________________________________________ __________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e65ddcf67efbc01ec957f3d8814844ec4b31f67a"},{"id":"q-31fbfde3ae4a","stem":"The following is one of the early complications of burn: a) Cushing ulcer b) Marjolin ulcer c) Curling’s ulcer d) Tropical ulcer e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9724bc11baef","stem":"The following are correct about burn wound except: a) Partial thickness burns heals by reepithilialization b) Second degree burns heals with minimal scarring and are painless c) First degree burn involve only the epidermis and is characterized by erytheme d) Superficial burns are often characterized by blister formation e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1546e29043d0","stem":"The following are major burn injuries except: a) Second degree burn of > 25% BSA in adults b) Second degree burn of > 25% BSA in children c) Second degree burn of < 15% BSA in adult d) Third degree burn of > 10% BSA e) Burn injury complicated by other major trauma","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e67d90708460","stem":"Considering the Closed Method of burn wound management the following statements are true except: a) An occlusive dressing is applied over the burn area b) Dressing is usually changed twice daily c) There is less pain and less heat loss d) The bacterial growth is not enhanced e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be1d50421aef","stem":"The following are true above chest tube insertion: a) Is done just below the rib above in the intercostals space b) 5% lignocaine is used as local anaesthetic c) For haemothorax is inserted in 7th intercostals space in the midaxillary line d) For Empyema thracis is inserted in the 2nd intercostals space midaxillary line e) 1% xylocane may be used","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5ee11c1b962a","stem":"The following are true about pneumothorax except: a) May be spontaneous or following trauma b) Is associated with sudden chest pain c) Is characterized by hyperresonance d) May be associated with ventilatory and circulatory compromise e) If it is less than 25% will need tube thoracostomy","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-489d692d9494","stem":"The following items can be sterilized using chemical method: a) Surgical linen b) Esophagascope c) Dressing gauze d) Laryngascope e) ‘b’ and ‘d’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-051e17a8d805","stem":"The following method of sterilization is used in industries for Mass production of sterile medical products: a) Chemical b) Autoclaving c) Gamma radiation d) Hot air oven e) None of the above 4","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3141035f9cc0","stem":"Shock is defined as the circumstance of insufficient oxygen delivery to the tissues. Etiology of shock in surgical patients is: a) Vomiting b) Dirrhoea c) Burn injury d) All of the above e) All of the above except (b)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a3313165da2d","stem":"Hypovolemic shock can be seen in patients with the following conditions except: a) Intestinal obstruction low and high b) Burn injury > 30% c) Excessive sweating d) Ruptured visceral e) Moderate Head injury","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-52fafba7a6c4","stem":"One of the following sentences is not correct: a) Sterilization is the destruction all living microbes b) Disinfection is the removal of harmful microbes not usually including bacterial spores c) Sterilized equipment is essential for all procedures involving a break in atmosphere, skin and mucous membrane d) Surgical asepsis refers to practices that render and keep objects and areas free from micro-organisms e) Contamination is the process by which an instrument is soiled with micro organisms","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb81a4b82e7a","stem":"A 15 years old girl noticed a painless little puffiness around the left ankle which she says disappears after a night’s rest, only to return at a late stage of the next day. She reports that it has gradually progressed slowly. Denies any skin changes, pigmentation or ulceration. What is the most likely clinical diagnosis? a) Recurrent cellulites b) Lymphoedema praecox c) Filariasis of the Left leg d) Secondary lymphoedema e) None of the above 5","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5a23f7c9308","stem":"Which of the following is true about secondary lymphoedema? a) May follow involvement of lymph channels or lymph nodes by neoplastic, inflammatory process or surgical procedure b) Tends to be localized and develops much more rapidly c) Recurrent cellulites is not uncommon d) All of the above e) Only ‘a’ and ‘b’ is true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-422a442c312f","stem":"Which of the following statements regarding abscess is true? a) Pyaemic abscess is the commonest variety b) Cardinal features of acute inflammation are usually present c) Fluctuation test becomes negative when the pus becomes superficial d) Usually treatment with parenteral antibiotics is enough e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2210254525ea","stem":"The following are examples of an acquired fistula except: a) Fistula-in-ano b) Thyroglossal fistula c) Arteriovenous fistula d) Branchial fistula e) All of the above are acquired fistulae","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c6f9a28eba45","stem":"The following is true about incarcerated hernia: a) Has compromised blood supply b) Is not reducible c) Is reducible d) There is intestinal obstruction e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b565420f51d","stem":"The following type of hernia appears in the Hesselbach’s triangle: a) Richter hernia b) Fermoral hernia c) Indirect hernia d) Direct hernia e) Umblical hernia","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2dbdcca8ff14","stem":"The following is true about groin hernias in females: a) Inguinal hernia is the commonest b) Fermoral hernia is the commonest c) Direct hernia is the most common d) Males are prone to develop fermoral hernia compared to females e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-356564dfb4e8","stem":"The following is true about groin hernia: 6 a) Direct inguinal hernia produce fewer symptoms than indirect hernia b) Indirect hernia is less likely to strangulate than the direct hernia c) There is no weakness of the transversalis fascia in direct inguinal hernia d) The direct hernia exits through the processus vaginalis e) All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-faf213200827","stem":"A hernia that descends into the scrotum is most likely to be: a) Direct inguinal hernia b) Fermoral hernia c) Richters hernia d) Indirect inguinal hernia e) All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2e8b47f10ca","stem":"The following factors may cause hernia reccurance after repair: a) Poor surgical technique b) Overlooking an indirect sac c) Failure to repair the fascial defect securely d) Defect in Collagen Synthesis e) All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Abdominal Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Abdominal Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0f7db03bdae4","stem":"The following are complications of acute appendicitis except: a) Perforation b) Peritonitis c) Abscess formation d) Pylephlebitis e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a5c96446213e","stem":"The following are present in acute appendicitis except: a) Abdominal pain in left iliac fossa b) Anorexia c) Nausea d) Vomiting e) All of the above are true","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-252b02f5ea60","stem":"The following complication of acute appendicitis is managed expectantly: a) Peritonitis b) Appendicular abscess c) Appendicular mass d) Perforation e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ba7a4d830c6e","stem":"The following aerobic bacteria is frequently isolated from perforated appendicitis: a) Bacteroides fragilis b) Other bacteroides species c) Lactobacilus d) Fusobactoerium species e) Escherichia coli","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-902b91c6df17","stem":"One of the following skin conditions is a pre-malignant condition: a) Naevus flammeus (port-wine stain) b) Capillary haemangioma c) Naevi d) Squamous cell papilloma e) All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-222541d72add","stem":"Papillomas are: a) Are simple overgrowths of all layers of the skin b) May arise from epithelial surfaces either the epidermis or the mucosa c) May be treated by excision or application of formaldehyde d) Are benign tumours of glandular tissues e) ‘a’ ‘b’ and ‘c’ are the correct answers","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f682da50f613","stem":"The following is a vascular tumour: a) Haemangioma b) Dermoid cyst c) Trichilemmal (pillar) cyst d) Epidermal cyst e) All of the above are correct answers","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dc2ae736d566","stem":"These are cutaneous neural (neuromas) tumours except: a) Acrochordons (skin tags) b) Neurofibromas c) Neurilemmomas (Schwannomas) d) Granular cell tumours e) ‘a’ and ‘b’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neurology","topicOriginal":"Neurology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neurology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb1312877f63","stem":"Aneurysm: a) Is defined as a degree of arterial narrowing sufficient to produce a pressure drop. b) Is an irreversible dilatation of an artery to at least one and one half times its diameter c) True aneurysm involves all layers of the arterial wall 8 d) False aneurysm involves only a portion of the vessel wall or surrounding tissues e) All are correct except ‘a’","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1912a5cdbda","stem":"Arterial vasospasm occurs in all of the following clinical conditions except: a) Arteriosclerosis b) Raynaud’s syndrome c) Acrocyanosis d) Drug induced e.g. ergotamine, oral contraceptives e) Scleroderma","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e69b3007a4bd","stem":"These are characteristic features of dry gangrene except: a) Deprivation of arterial blood supply b) Deprivation of venous drainage c) Venous outflow remains unimpeded d) Occlusion of a vessel is by a chronic process or an acute process e) Colour changes from greenish to finally black","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e2c9b4447ea0","stem":"The following are the indications for amputation except: a) Loss of viability due to non-reconstructible ischaemia b) Trauma that is sufficient extensive to preclude repair c) Tumours d) Extensive infection that does not respond to conservative measures or cause septicaemia e) Established contracture of the limb","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5573373734c0","stem":"The following are present in patient with thyrotoxicosis except: a) Nervousness b) Heat intolerance c) Muscular weakness d) Polyuria e) None of the above 9","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-269a3788b85f","stem":"In patients with hyperthyrodism the following may be present except: a) Goitre b) Warm moist skin c) Thyroid thrill and bruit d) Gynaecomastia e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b88e02a5563","stem":"With regard to the treatment of thyrotoxicosis, the following are effective options: a) Anthithyroid drugs b) Radioactive iodine c) Thyroidectomy d) All of the above e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c154813099fd","stem":"An ulcer with Rolled edge is most likely to be: a) Epithelioma b) Rodent ulcer c) Tuberculous ulcer d) Healing ulcer e) All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-de12b23e42d5","stem":"An abscess is a collection of pus. Bacteria which cause pus (Pyogenic Bacteria) reach the infected area by: a) Direct infection from outside b) Local extension from some adjacent focus of infection c) Lymphatics d) Blood stream e) All of the above 10","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-57072beb2513","stem":"The following techniques may allow surgical infections and collection of pus to be accurately diagnosed and clearly defined: a) Conventional radiology b) Isotope scanning c) Ultrasound d) Computed Axial Tomography e) All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3387026c1697","stem":"The principles of antibiotic prophylaxis: a) Choose antibiotics for the expected type of contamination b) Use antibiotics only if the risk of infection justifies their use c) Give antibiotics in appropriate doses and at appropriate times d) Stop dosing before the risk of side effects outweighs benefits e) All of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Infectious Diseases","topicOriginal":"Infectious Diseases","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Infectious Diseases","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-957efbf2efc7","stem":"Thrombosis of veins is predisposed to by Virchow’s triad. The following are the factors in the Virchow’s triad: a) Change in the vessel wall with damage to endothelium b) Increased coagulability of the blood c) Diminished rate of blood flow d) Anaemia e) ‘a’, ‘b’ and ‘c’ are correct","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9e9281b567ab","stem":"The following are the complications of varicose vein except: a) Thrombophlebitis b) Chronic dermatitis (eczema) c) Venous ulcer d) All of the above e) None of the above","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8cbf581bf8eb","stem":"The best diagnostic procedure to differentiate mechanical small bowel obstruction from peritonitis: a) Abdominal distension b) Peristalsis reduction on auscultation c) A palpable left lower quadrant abdominal mass d) Dehydration e) Abdominal tympany 11 SECTION II - SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-61e6ed973d01","stem":"Fluid and electrolute management in surgical patients is divided into three phases mention them: a) ____________________________________________________ b) _____________________________________________________ c) _____________________________________________________ 12","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d294a63fc559","stem":"Mention various methods of sterilization (about 4) of equipments and the use a) ______________________________________________________ b) _______________________________________________________ c) _______________________________________________________ d) _______________________________________________________","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d3226f9aad1","stem":"List 5 classical local signs of inflammation: a) __________________________________________________ b) __________________________________________________ c) ___________________________________________________ d) ___________________________________________________ e) ___________________________________________________ 15","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ef3a5b6e6be8","stem":"List the indications for admission in burn patient: a) ________________________________________________ b) ________________________________________________ c) ________________________________________________ d) ________________________________________________ e) ________________________________________________ f) ________________________________________________","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90d836f61b9d","stem":"Mention the indications for tube thoracostomy: a) ________________________________________________________ __ b) ________________________________________________________ __ c) ________________________________________________________ __ d) ________________________________________________________ __ e) ________________________________________________________ __ f) ________________________________________________________ __","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a1526d9889db","stem":"A patient with 60kgs body weight come with 6 hours old burn injury involving 40% of his body surface. Name the fluids that you will administer. Calculate the amount needed per 1st 24 hours and how is distributed. ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ 16 ___________________________________________________________ ___________________________________________________________ ________________________________________________________","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3ad37e6fb833","stem":"List down 4 of the 6 P’s of clinical manifestation of acute arterial occlusion: a) ________________________________________________________ ____ b) ________________________________________________________ ____ c) ________________________________________________________ ____ d) ________________________________________________________ _____","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-deb1373a7282","stem":"The spleen does all of the following EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serve as a reserve and storage site for platelets"},{"label":"B","text":"Trap red blood cell inclusions (Howell-Jolly bodies, etc.)"},{"label":"C","text":"Become a site for extramedullary hematopoiesis (EMH)"},{"label":"D","text":"Become enlarged in adults with sickle cell anemia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88b180d6370e","stem":"Immature white blood cells are readily seen in the peripheral blood in all of the following conditions EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukemoid reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelogenous leukemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dfda26bd68bb","stem":"8%, MCV 92 fL, platelet count 390,000/uL, and WBC count 190,000/uL with differential count 73 segs, 12 bands, 6 metamyelocytes, 2 myelocytes, 2 myeloblasts, and 5 lymphs. He has splenomegaly. The leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score is only 8. A bone marrow biopsy is performed. Which of the following findings would you most likely expect to be found in the biopsy","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sheets of plasma cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Numerous mature and immature myeloid cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomas that have many acid fast bacilli"},{"label":"D","text":"Numerous groups of megakaryocytes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0eb4891b2065","stem":"A 64-year-old man has noted the presence of several lumps on the right side of his neck for several months. These are firm, non-tender, movable lymph nodes palpable in right posterior cervical region. He does not have splenomegaly or hepatomegaly. He has a mild normocytic anemia, and his WBC count is normal. A lymph node biopsy shows numerous crowded follicles of small, monomorphic lymphocytes. This is most typical for","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Infectious mononucleosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive hyperplasia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b3de314f19d2","stem":"Which one of the following causes for anemia is LEAST likely to be associated with drug use","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Macrocytic anemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Aplastic anemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Microcytic anemia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bfaea9d3a14f","stem":"A 30-year-old man gives a history of a sore throat with fever followed by 6 weeks of malaise, during which time he develops lymphadenopathy. A lymph node biopsy shows a diffuse polyclonal hyperplasia. The most probable diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphocytic lymphoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin's disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Infectious mononucleosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Human immunodeficiency virus infection"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9a60c1d47fb","stem":"A 45-year-old man has had increasing abdominal discomfort with enlargement for the past two years. On physical examination, the spleen can be felt below the left costal margin. An abdominal CT scan reveals massive (3000 gm size) splenomegaly. Of the following conditions, the one most likely to cause this finding is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelofibrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Portal hypertension"},{"label":"C","text":"Infectious mononucleosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Hemochromatosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-602c4905f07f","stem":"A 63-year-old woman has had malaise and nausea for over a year. Her Hgb is 7.9 g/dL. Her stool is negative for occult blood. An upper GI endoscopy is performed and reveals loss of rugal folds. A gastric biopsy reveals mucosal atrophy. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely to be present","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased serum ferritin"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased reticulocyte count"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased neutrophil segmentation"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased MCV in RBC's"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-035d733cd0c0","stem":"A 5-year-old boy has become too tired to play with his friends. Whenever he falls, he gets a big bruise. A CBC shows: Hgb 9.2 g/dL, Hct 27.8%, MCV 91 fL, platelet count 101,000/microliter, and WBC count 12,128/microliter. A bone marrow biopsy is performed and shows nearly 100% cellularity with replacement by primitive cells that have large nuclei with delicate chromatin and indistinct nucleoli with scanty cytoplasm. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myelogenous leukemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin's disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Leukemoid reaction"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fb96413d1c9","stem":"Of the following findings, the one that will be most likely to occur with extravascular, rather than intravascular, hemolysis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased reticulocyte count"},{"label":"B","text":"Splenomegaly"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute renal failure"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased serum haptoglobin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-346a049dcf7b","stem":"A 32-year-old man has had worsening headaches for the past 2 months. On physical examination he is afebrile. He has no lymphadenopathy or hepatoplenomegaly. A head CT scan reveals a 3 cm mass lesion to the right of midline next to the lateral ventricle. A stereotaxic brain biopsy is performed and a diagnosis of diffuse large B cell lymphoma is made. A bone marrow biopsy shows slightly decreased cellularity of all cell lines. Which of the following laboratory test findings is this patient most likely to have","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated terminal deoxyribonucleotidyl transferase"},{"label":"B","text":"Bence-Jones proteinuria"},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated serum IgM"},{"label":"D","text":"HIV-1 RNA of 8000 copies/mL"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58b1d455c28f","stem":"A 10-year-old child is found to have a mandibular mass that produces facial deformity. This mass has been increasing in size over the past 8 months. The mass on biopsy reveals a monotonous pattern of small non-cleaved lymphocytes. Which of the following statements is inconsistent with this finding","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epstein-Barr virus infection is present."},{"label":"B","text":"This neoplasm is of T-cell origin."},{"label":"C","text":"Though it is fast-growing, leukemic transformation is uncommon."},{"label":"D","text":"Children and young adults are usually affected by this neoplasm."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7df55aa8b34b","stem":"A 45-year-old plethoric man presents with headache and dizziness. Physical examination reveals no lymphadenopathy. A bone marrow biopsy demonstrates hypercellularity with all cell lines increased in number. Which set of findings is most likely to be present","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"WBC differential count: 80 segs, 14 bands, 4 lymphs, 1 mono, 1 eosinophil"},{"label":"B","text":"CBC with Hgb 20 gm/dl, Hct 61%, and MCV 92 fL"},{"label":"C","text":"CBC with Hgb 10 gm/dl, Hct 30%, MCV 85 fL, Platelet count 3,000 per microliter"},{"label":"D","text":"WBC differential count: 59 segs, 3 bands, 18 lymphs, 5 monos, 15 eosinophils"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d787c98af033","stem":"A 50-year-old woman has noted increasing abdominal enlargement for the past year. On physical examination, there is a fluid wave, but no tenderness. Bowel sounds are present. She has no vaginal bleeding. An abdominal ultrasound reveals bilateral 10 and 7 cm adnexal masses. At surgery you find bilateral mass lesions of the ovaries which are unilocular, filled with watery fluid, and covered with papillary excrescences on all surfaces. These masses are probably","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulosa-theca cell tumors"},{"label":"B","text":"Mature cystic teratomas"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrosarcoma with metastases"},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral serous cystadenocarcinomas"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91bc48f89c9f","stem":"A 64-year-old woman has had itching with irritation of the vulvar region, along with vaginal dryness. A biopsy of a 1 x 2 cm greyish vulvar lesion microscopically shows epithelial thinning, dermal fibrosis, and perivascular chronic inflammation. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen sclerosus"},{"label":"C","text":"Condyloma acuminatum"},{"label":"D","text":"Adenosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c792336aee93","stem":"Following an abnormal Pap smear in a 38-year-old woman, a cervical biopsy is performed. A microscopic section shows dysplasia involving the full thickness of the cervical epithelium. Your diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia III"},{"label":"B","text":"Severe chronic cervicitis with Herpes simplex"},{"label":"C","text":"Previous diethylstilbesterol (DES) exposure"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocervical adenocarcinoma"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d9af185a3b62","stem":"A 43-year-old woman has noted menstrual periods that have been exceptionally heavy, lasting 6 to 8 days, for several months. She has noted occasional minor intermenstrual bleeding. The uterine cervix appears normal, and a Pap smear shows no abnormal cells. Pelvic examination reveals that the uterus is enlarged to twice normal size and is somewhat nodular. There are no adnexal masses. What pathologic condition is most likely to be present","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrial carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Leiomyomata"},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopic pregnancy"},{"label":"D","text":"Endometriosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b479e802ff7","stem":"Two weeks following delivery of a term infant, a 25-year-old woman still has a vaginal discharge consisting of dark brown, foul-smelling material. She has a fever. On pelvic examination, the uterus is slightly enlarged and she has pelvic tenderness. Her serum beta- HCG is negative. Which of the following conditions is most likely to be present","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invasive mole"},{"label":"B","text":"Degenerating leiomyoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Endometriosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Retained products of conception"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c970c5de5410","stem":"A 35-year-old woman has had dull pelvic pain for several months. A pelvic examination reveals a normal appearing cervix, a normal sized uterus, and a large tender right adnexal mass. Ultrasound reveals an 8 cm mass involving the right adnexal region that appears cystic and fluid-filled. At laparotomy, there are many filmy fibrous adhesions in the pelvis. The mass is excised and examined in surgical pathology, where it is found to have a thin wall and is filled with purulent exudate. Microscopically, there are thin remnants of fallopian tube and ovary comprising the wall of the mass. Which of the following infectious agents is most likely to have produced these findings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichomonas vaginalis"},{"label":"B","text":"Neisseria gonorrheae"},{"label":"C","text":"Candida albicans"},{"label":"D","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6c8d3b2a114e","stem":"5%, MCV 72 fL, platelet count 158,000/microliter, and WBC count 7150/microliter. You elicit a history of vaginal bleeding for the past year. You refer the patient to a gynecologist, who performs an endometrial biopsy. The biopsy demonstrates atypical adenomatous hyperplasia. This condition most likely resulted from","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Gynecology","topicOriginal":"Gynecology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human papillomavirus infection"},{"label":"B","text":"Long term use of an intrauterine contraceptive device"},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic endometritis"},{"label":"D","text":"Unopposed estrogenic stimulation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Gynecology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b2ac20e3b1ea","stem":"A 25-year-old man has normal descended testes that appear normal in size on physical examination. If only Sertoli cells were present in the seminiferous tubules on biopsy, then this patient would most likely have","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infertility"},{"label":"B","text":"Orchitis"},{"label":"C","text":"A Leydig cell tumor"},{"label":"D","text":"A mildly decreased sperm count"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a9ea09bb222","stem":"A 30-year-old man had a feeling of heaviness in his left testis. Physical examination revealed the presence of a solid mass on the left, while the right testis appeared normal. An ultrasound revealed a 4 cm solid mass of the left testis. There is a palpable left inguinal lymph node. Laboratory findings included a serum beta-HCG of 5 IU/L and alpha- fetoprotein of 2 ng/mL. The testis is excised and on sectioning reveals a firm, lobulated light tan mass without hemorrhage or necrosis. He receives radiation therapy. Histologically, this mass is most likely to have the pattern of a(an)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Obstetrics","topicOriginal":"Obstetrics","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choriocarcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Embryonal carcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Seminoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Leydig cell tumor"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Obstetrics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d7204fef3d6","stem":"A 19-year-old man notes the sudden onset of severe discomfort in his scrotum late one evening. No position is comfortable. Aspirin has no effect. In the emergency room, the physician notes that the left testis is slightly enlarged and exquisitely tender. There is no inguinal adenopathy. No other findings are noted. His vital signs include temperature 36 C., respirations 22, pulse 80, and blood pressure 100/65 mm Hg. Which of the following conditions is most likely to be present","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Varicocele"},{"label":"B","text":"Ureteral lithiasis"},{"label":"C","text":"Spermatic cord torsion"},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrocele"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59ddc0208b2b","stem":"On physical examination of a 35-year-old man, you palpate a left inguinal mass which is consistent with a cryptorchid testis. The right testis is palpated in the scrotum and is of normal size. What should you do about the cryptorchid testis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Put it into the scrotum surgically"},{"label":"B","text":"Remove it along with the opposite testis"},{"label":"C","text":"Remove it"},{"label":"D","text":"Put the patient on testosterone therapy"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fb9ee2c68119","stem":"An eczematous area about 1 cm in diameter has been present for months on the skin of the areola of the right breast of a 39-year-old woman. There is no palpable lump in this breast. Biopsy of this lesion reveals large cells at the dermal-epidermal junction that stain positively for mucin. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraductal carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Paget's disease of breast"},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatophyte infection"},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammatory carcinoma"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c348b41e2fe5","stem":"A 25-year-old woman palpates a left breast \"lump\" on self-examination. Fine needle aspiration yields cells that appear benign, but the lesion persists. A biopsy is taken and a diagnosis of fibrocystic changes is made. All of the following histologic features may be expected in this lesion EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sclerosing adenosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Ductal epithelial proliferation"},{"label":"C","text":"Cartilaginous metaplasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Apocrine metaplasia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d540c5f657f","stem":"Which of the following pathologic changes on biopsy of a \"lump\" of the left breast most strongly suggests an increased risk for subsequent development of breast carcinoma in a 45-year-old woman","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lobular epithelial hyperplasia"},{"label":"B","text":"Sclerosing adenosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Fat necrosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple cysts"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c505930a8bfd","stem":"A 52-year-old woman feels a lump in her right breast. The mass is not freely movable and feels quite hard. A fine needle aspirate is performed and cells are present consistent with carcinoma. Which of the following features of her carcinoma would suggest a worse prognosis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Neoplasia","topicOriginal":"Neoplasia","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estrogen receptor positivity"},{"label":"B","text":"Family history of breast carcinoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Axillary lymph node metastases"},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of an in-situ component"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Neoplasia","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2e6b4706f75","stem":"49-year-old woman notes increasing size to her right breast over the past year. The breast is not painful, but the heaviness causes some discomfort. The overlying skin and nipple appear normal. There is no nipple discharge. There is no axillary lymphadenopathy. Mammography reveals a solid 12-cm circumscribed mass. The mass is biopsied, and microscopically the lesion includes a cellular stromal component along with an epithelial component. This lesion is most likely a","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary carcinoma"},{"label":"B","text":"Hamartoma"},{"label":"C","text":"Phyllodes tumor"},{"label":"D","text":"Fibroadenoma TRUE / FALSE QUESTIONS"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Breast and Endocrine Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-006decd804c0","stem":"Hypospadia is the name of an abnormal urethral orifice at the ventral side of the penis. T","typ