 2004)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mercaptopurine"},{"label":"B","text":"Dipivefrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Enalapril"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"E","text":"Linezolid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-004652bfe087","stem":"The process by which the amount of a drug in the body decreases after administration but before entering the systemic circulation is called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excretion"},{"label":"B","text":"First pass effect"},{"label":"C","text":"First order elimination"},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolism"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-aa34fb89e27c","stem":"The most general term for the process by which the amount of active drug in the body is reduced after absorption into the systemic circulation is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excretion"},{"label":"B","t