ce will be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 L/hr"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 L/hr"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 L/hr"},{"label":"D","text":"20 L/hr"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-70517e83d78b","stem":"A drug following first order kinetics is being administered by constant i.v. infusion at a rate of 10 mg/ min. Its steady state plasma concentration is 2 mg/min. If the dose rate is increased to 20 mg/dl, what will be the new steady state plasma concentration?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 mg/dl"},{"label":"B","text":"4 mg/dl"},{"label":"C","text":"3 mg/dl"},{"label":"D","text":"1 mg/dl"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af6ff92b7ca0","stem":"Ram Prashad is admitted to Guru Teg Bahadur Hospital with respiratory infection for which antibiotic tobramycin is ordered. The clearance and Vd of tobramycin in him are 160 ml/min and 40 L, respectively. If you wish to give Ram Prashad an intravenous loading dose to achieve the therapeutic plasma concentration of 4 mg/L rapidly, how much should be given?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 mg"},{"label":"B","text":"10 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"115.2 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"160 mg"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b913879aa5eb","stem":"A 30 year old patient on digoxin therapy has developed digitalis toxicity. The plasma digoxin level is 4 ng/ ml. Renal function is normal and the plasma t1/2 for digoxin in this patient is 1.6 days. How long should you withhold digoxin in order to reach a safer yet probably therapeutic level of 1 ng/ml?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.6 days"},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 days"},{"label":"C","text":"3.2 days"},{"label":"D","text":"4.8 days"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7bdbd5e04964","stem":"An old man enters the hospital with myocardial infarction and a severe ventricular arrhythmia. The antiarrhythmic drug chosen has a narrow therapeutic window. The minimum toxic plasma concentration is 1.5 times the minimum therapeutic plasma concentration. The half life is 6 hrs. It is essential to maintain the plasma concentration above the minimum therapeutic level to prevent a possible lethal arrhythmia. Of the following, the most appropriate dosing regimen would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Once a day"},{"label":"B","text":"Twice a day"},{"label":"C","text":"Four times a day"},{"label":"D","text":"Constant intravenous infusion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f0ff2177e424","stem":"A young male Kallu is brought to the hospital with severe asthma. The pharmacokinetics of theophylline include the following parameters: Vd = 35 L; CL = 48 ml/min; half life is 8 hrs. If an intravenous infusion of theophylline is started at a rate of 0.48 mg/min, how long will it take to reach 93.75% of the final steady state?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Respiratory","topicOriginal":"Respiratory","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Approximately 48 min"},{"label":"B","text":"Approximately 5.8 hrs"},{"label":"C","text":"Approximately 8 hrs"},{"label":"D","text":"Approximately 32 hrs"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Respiratory","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-49c0e3f401df","stem":"A patient requires an infusion of procainamide. Its half life is 2 hrs. The infusion is begun at 9 AM At 1 PM on the same day, the blood concentration is found to be 3 mg/L.What is the probable steady state concentration after 2 days of infusion?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mg/L"},{"label":"B","text":"4 mg/L"},{"label":"C","text":"6 mg/L"},{"label":"D","text":"15 mg/L"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dcd7ba25d1ff","stem":"A volunteer Ram will receive a new drug in a phase I clinical trial. The clearance and the volume of distribution of the drug in Ram are 1.386 L/hr and 80 L respectively. The half life of the drug in him would be approximately","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"83 hr"},{"label":"B","text":"77 hr"},{"label":"C","text":"40 hr"},{"label":"D","text":"0.02 hr"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b49c89a681ea","stem":"Drug X is normally administered to patients at a rate of 50 mg/hour. Elimination of the drug X from body takes place as: - Hepatic Metabolism 10% - Biliary Secretion 10% - Renal Excretion 80% This drug has to be administered to a 65 years old patient Uttaam Singh, with a GFR of 60 ml/min. (assuming normal GFR is 120ml/min). Liver and biliary functions are normal in this patient. What should be the dose rate of drug X in this patient? https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ tahir99 - UnitedVRG 27 27 General Pharmacology General Pharmacology General Pharmacology","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Gastrointestinal","topicOriginal":"Gastrointestinal","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 mg/hour"},{"label":"B","text":"30 mg/hr"},{"label":"C","text":"25 mg/hr"},{"label":"D","text":"100 mg/hr."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Gastrointestinal","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-138a6ba6ad3d","stem":"Zero order kinetics is followed by all of the following drugs EXCEPT: (DPG 2005)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"B","text":"Barbiturates"},{"label":"C","text":"Alcohol"},{"label":"D","text":"Theophylline"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-630a13513a2e","stem":"Time required to reach the steady state after a dosage regimen depends on: (MPPG 2003)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Route of administration"},{"label":"B","text":"Half life of a drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Dosage interval"},{"label":"D","text":"Dose of drug"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f2858364dda","stem":"Zero order kinetic is shown by all EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"High dose salicylates\b (Karnataka 2002, MPPG 2001)"},{"label":"B","text":"Phenytoin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ethanol"},{"label":"D","text":"Methotrexate"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ec9126cd0866","stem":"Zero order kinetics occur in following drug with high dose: (DNB 2007, 2003, 2006)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenytoin and Theophylline"},{"label":"B","text":"Digoxin and Propranol"},{"label":"C","text":"Amiloride and Probenecid"},{"label":"D","text":"Lithium and Theophylline"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-608d8d5f76ea","stem":"About first order kinetics true statement is: (Bihar 2005)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clearance remains constant"},{"label":"B","text":"Fixed amount of the drug is eliminated"},{"label":"C","text":"Half life increase with dose"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased clearance with increasing dose Pharmacodynamics and pharmacogenetics"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-df66ceed98d2","stem":"All of the following can cause SLE-like syndrome except: (AIIMS May 2013,2012)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydralazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Sulphonamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c87bf59e8a5f","stem":"The neurotransmitters; nor-adrenaline, adrenaline and dopamine act through which of the following receptors? (AIIMS Nov 2011)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Renal and Urinary","topicOriginal":"Renal and Urinary","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single pass transmembrane receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Four pass transmembrane receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Seven pass transmembrane receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Ligand gated receptors"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Renal and Urinary","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-293a639a1c55","stem":"Which one of the following statements best describes the mechanism of action of insulin on target cells? (Delhi PG 2011)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrinology","topicOriginal":"Endocrinology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin binds to cytoplasmic receptor molecule and is transferred as a hormone receptor complex to the nucleus where it acts to modulate gene expression. https://kat.cr/user/Blink99/ 28 Review of Pharmacology General Pharmacology"},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin binds to a receptor molecule on the outer surface of the plasma membrane and the hormone receptor complex activates adenylate cyclase through the Gs protein."},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin binds to a transmembrane receptor at the outer surface of the plasma membrane, which activates the tyrosine kinase that is the cytosolic domain of the receptor."},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin enters the cell and causes the release of calcium ions from intracellular stores."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrinology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-345beecc659a","stem":"True about G protein coupled receptors is: (AIIMS May 2008)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"G proteins bind to hormones on the cell surface"},{"label":"B","text":"All the three subunits alpha, beta and gamma should bind to each other for G proteins to act"},{"label":"C","text":"G proteins act as inhibitory and excitatory because of difference in alpha subunit"},{"label":"D","text":"G protein is bound to GTP in resting state"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-db23c5856459","stem":"Which drug is not metabolized by acetylation? (AIIMS May 2008 AIIMS Nov 2006, AIIMS May 2003)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"B","text":"Dapsone"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydralazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclopropamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24111ecc3d59","stem":"All are second messengers except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclic amp\b (AIIMS NOV 2008, 2012)"},{"label":"B","text":"Guanylyl cyclase"},{"label":"C",